right now for morning rounds our look at  the medical news of the week and we  begin with a surprising and concerning  rise in colon cancer death rates among  younger Americans a new research letter  published by the American Medical  Association examined mortality rates for  those aged 20 to 50 for the major  finding from 2004 to 2014 the colon  cancer death rate for white Americans  increased by around 14 percent or  roughly a 1.4 percent annual increase  statistics on African Americans actually  showed a decline here to discuss the  research is CBS News contributor dr.  Tara Narula tere are there signs  symptoms that people should keep in mind  here this is very important for people  to understand this is a very common  cancer it's the third leading cause of  cancer death in women the second leading  cause of cancer death in men estimated  that it will take about 50,000 lives  this year so while many times it can  remain asymptomatic there are things  that you can look for for example if you  notice any bright red blood in your  stool or change in the stool where it's  a little bit darker you have change in  your bowel habits so either diarrhea or  constipation belly pain unusual fatigue  weakness or loss of appetite and then  anemia sometimes that's the only sign a  low blood count or anemia that will pick  up that something is wrong  now there are certain conditions that  can predispose you genetically if you  have a strong family history if you have  other hereditary conditions or genetic  conditions and then inflammatory bowel  disease like Crohn's are all sort of  colitis but most important to remember  is that there are lifestyle risk factors  things that you can do yourself that can  prevent or help to reduce your risk so  for example diabetes out heavy alcohol  use smoking people who eat a diet that's  high in red or processed meat who don't  exercise as much who are overweight or  obese  all of those things will increase your  risk of developing course as Dana  mentioned the rate actually declined  among African Americans so do we know I  this affects some groups more than  others we don't really but certainly  there are or seem to be differences  racially and ethnically one of the  theories that has come about is this  idea that the microbiome may have an  effect so the microbiome what is it it's  the trillions of bacteria that live in  our gut over a thousand species and that  somehow through our changes in diet  exposures were altering our microbiome  and that this may be affecting us in  differential ways so that certain  ethnicities or races have different  micro biomes and certain bacteria may  actually promote the development of  cancer but important to understand from  the study we talked about  african-americans in general they are  actually at highest risk of developing  colon cancer highest risk of dying from  colon cancer they develop cancer a  little bit differently tends to be more  right-sided it could be at younger ages  more aggressive so actually the American  College of gastroenterology recommends  that african-americans consider being  screened as early as 45 our next topic  deals with another serious health issue  stroke according to the Centers for  Disease Control someone in the United  States suffers a stroke every 40 seconds  a new study found that while stroke  rates are going down for men that might  not be the case for women CBS News chief  medical correspondent dr. Jon LaPook  took a closer look in 2013 Diana  Hardiman was 30 years old a vegetarian a  nonsmoker  a surfer and the picture of health until  she had a stroke the paralysis ended up  seeping down from my arm to my leg  leaving the whole right side of my body  basically immobile I thought maybe I'm  becoming paralyzed or potentially seeing  death it was terrifying Hardiman is an  example of a puzzling and concerning  trend this week's report found from 1999  to 2005 the incidence of stroke declined  in both men and women but from 2005 to  2010 while the rates among men continued  to drop they stayed the same for women  dr. Katherine rexrode of Brigham and  Women's Hospital says risk factors for  stroke  such as obesity high blood pressure an  irregular heartbeat and diabetes may for  some reason affect women differently  than men  diabetes is a strong risk factor for  stroke in both men and women but in  women the risk is 26% higher than in men  with died  Hardiman recovered and went back to her  gourmet ice-cream business in Brooklyn  but last June she had a second stroke I  had to stop being CEO of my ice cream  business and start being CEO of my  health and put that as my priority which  I did this time doctors found the cause  a small hole in her heart and repaired  it when I was told that I had a stroke  I've always associated it with the  elderly something that your grandmother  has but never thought it would be  something that would happen to me I  think a lot of people would feel that  way it's surprising to read that  according to the CDC stroke kills twice  as many women as breast cancer is that  correct this is something that needs to  be on every woman's radar and I don't  think it is as a cardiologist we talk  about it a lot and women are really  unaware that this is the third leading  cause of death for women one out of five  women will have a stroke in their  lifetime and more women than men are  affected by stroke and as you saw you  know it is debilitating when you  suddenly cannot see cannot walk cannot  move part of your body can't speak  this is life-altering and so we're just  starting to really understand more about  this in 2014 is when the first  guidelines are really published about  women and Stroke and we learned that  there are risk factors that kind of tend  to affect women more than men that are  more prevalent in women for example  hypertension a big risk factor for  stroke  in addition things like migraine with  aura depression and anxiety and stress  more prevalent in women then there are  sex specific risk factors like pregnancy  gestational diabetes preeclampsia oral  contraceptive use as well as hormone  replacement therapy again all of these  raising the risk of stroke so really  important for women to not only  recognize the risk but realize that 80%  of strokes are preventable by looking at  their lifestyle and so in improving  things like exercise diet not smoking  monitoring blood pressure checking their  cholesterol monitoring blood sugar so  there's a lot we can do as women to  prevent this totally unaware of that I  mean I was associated more with men than  women I'm just stunned by those numbers  all right finally the ongoing battle  against antibiotic resistant bacteria  research published by the national  academy of  show the development of a test that  helps doctors better treat your  infection in the test only takes around  a half hour to produce a result so this  is really great right so you go to the  doctor you have a urine infection for  example and the doctor says well I'm not  sure if this antibiotic is really gonna  work for you but I'm gonna give it to  you anyway and so usually the tests to  see if your particular bacteria is  resistant or sensitive takes about one  to two days this would shorten that time  to about ten to thirty minutes so while  you're in the office they can test your  urine for example you can do it right  there right away  and then tell you here's the perfect  antibiotic that is perfect for the  bacteria you have and it avoids in the  bigger picture the creation of  antibiotic resistance which is what  happens when you give what we call  big-gun antibiotics that kill tons of  bacteria but you don't really need for  the infection exactly dr. taryn aruna  thank you
 

